    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This study investigates the influence of pressure dependent fluid and stress-sensitive rock properties on pressure transient analysis. Single phase flow through the porous medium is considered. The flow equation governing this variable-property flow problem is nonlinear, and no closed form analytical solution is available. The integration of this equation was carried out by finite difference techniques. Behavior of this transient flow problem in terms of a dimensionless pseudopressure m (1,^), is essentially the same as that of the conventional constant-property liquid flow theory expressed by dimensionless pressure p (Ijt^), for all practical production rates. Five different data sets of rock and fluid properties were used, and it was found that for a bounded system, after the exterior boundary affects production, the deviation of the m-.(l,t,.) solution from the p_.(l,t~) is characteristic for each data set. The extent of this deviation can be predicted by a simple graphical integration solution. This research also considers the effect of a finite and infinitesimal skin, and wellbore storage on oil well testing. In drawdown, injection, buildup and fall-off tests, after the short-time effect of the wellbore storage dies out, the flow capacity (or injection capacity), the wellbore condition, and the average reservoir pressure can be determined accurately if the results are expressed as a function of the normalized pseudopressure, m (p). When the results of these well tests are analyzed as a function of the pressure itself as is normally done, significant errors are found in the above three items of data.An easy method is presented to predict the wellbore and average reservoir pressure for a fixed production rate. This method considers pressure dependency of the fluid properties and the stress sensitivity of the rock properties.This work also shows how these variable properties affect water influx theory. Constant rate cases for both radial and linear systems of either finite or infinite extent are considered. For finite systems with constant exterior boundary pressure equal to p^, and also for the infinite systems, the solutions, if expressed in terms of m~(lst~)5 are nearly identical with classic p_.(l,t.p.) constant property solutions. For bounded systems, after the outer boundary affects the water movement, differences are found between m^Cljt-^) and the classic p^Cljt^) solutions. For the cases of constant pressure at the inner boundary, it was found that the cumulative influx, Q,., for both radial and linear infinite systems was a good linearization that compared well with the classic cumulative influx, Qy,, solutions. Differences between Q^ and QD are found for constant pressure cases in bounded systems. No simple means was found to correct for these differences.",
